"lists":[{"id":168502129438589,"name":"RYM Top — Pop — Adult Contemporary","rank":102,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":1}}],"members":[],"member_of":[],"genres":[{"id":9343125461845,"type":"genre","name":"Adult Contemporary","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":233,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":133646791193319,"type":"genre","name":"Laurel Canyon Scene","n":5},{"id":9343125461845,"type":"genre","name":"Adult Contemporary","n":3},{"id":136113600818764,"type":"genre","name":"Yacht Rock","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":98497937254844,"name":"Cantautores de los setenta","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1970,"end":1983,"desc":"James Taylor se convierte, misteriosamente, en el prototipo del cantautor de los setenta; Carole King, Carly Simon y Don McLean, sobrevalorados. Las innovaciones llegan del oeste: Randy Newman y sus viñetas urbanas con fusión de estilos populares, Shawn Phillips, J.J. Cale y su estilo «laid-back», Gram Parsons, Emmylou Harris, Roger McGuinn, Jackson Browne (Late For The Sky) y, sobre todo, Tom Waits, discípulo de Beefheart y cronista de marginados (Swordfishtrombones, Rain Dogs). Excéntricos como Todd Rundgren y Annette Peacock; en Gran Bretaña, Peter Hammill, Anthony Moore, Richard Thompson, Robert Calvert, John Cale, Kevin Coyne, Elton John y los ex Beatles. El realismo de Bruce Springsteen, verdadero heredero de Woody Guthrie y del blues, y la escuela italiana de cantautores (De André, Guccini, Battisti, Sorrenti).","color":"#a3e635","family":"Folk y cantautores","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#songwriters-70s"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":111069724522673,"artist":"Carly Simon","artist_id":224041277995319,"title":"No Secrets","year":1972,"ess":0,"yt":"P6gnvqMln1Y","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Cantautores de los setenta","scene_id":98497937254844,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#songwriters-70s","chapters":["Sección 4.3"],"pages":[[1,82,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":82,"text":"…whose solo album Tapestry (1971) was hailed as a revolution when, in fact, was still a collection of melodic pop songs. Even more over-rated was Carly Simon, whose No Secrets (1972) propelled her to the top of the musical and the feminist movement. Don McLean contributed to the renewal with the…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":86,"text":"…(1972), Daniel (1973), Goodbye Yellow Brick Road (1973), Don't Go Breaking My Heart (1976), etc. Realism 1973-74 If James Taylor, Carole King and Carly Simon did little to emancipate the artist, they certainly did a lot to bring her/him closer to the audience. The identification of the star with the…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Mystery of \"You're So Vain\" Target","body":"Carly Simon has famously kept the full identity of the \"you\" in her song \"You're So Vain\" a closely guarded secret for over half a century. While she's ruled out James Taylor and Mick Jagger, she did reveal that one verse refers to Warren Beatty, but not the entire song. The executive who paid $50,000 at a charity auction to learn the secret was sworn to secrecy. \"Carly Simon has spent more than half a century teasing us about who your so vain is about.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Here’s the Beef Edition Part 1","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Here’s the Beef Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4ef3154465cd600c4ef5"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best New Artist (1972)","body":"Nominated.","year":1972,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.